  "note": "What each bird's cell says (round K13 of the ring). `big` is the quantity the bird itself biases, read off the spell the theme is planned under: a control of the style (`control`), a field of derive() (`derive`), or a reading of the plan the bird leans (`plan`). `bands` segment it, rising: each band holds up to the threshold its `upTo` names, and the last holds the rest. A threshold is never a number typed here: `window` is a family's own `when` on that control in the style's composition (family, variant or null, control, min|max), and `record` is the edge of what the fourteen golden themes reach on the bird (HOUSE -/+ HOUSE_OUTER, spell.ts). `sub` is the seed's locked reading, which the bird does not bias, said as a subtitle with its role. `reads` is what the cell's whole reading is computed from, by the name the spell layer biases it under: `derive.<field>` for a field of derive(), `control.<name>` for a control of the style, `list.<id>` for a candidate list: the ring's dotted lines come from it, and tools/check.ts holds it to the measured matrix (tools/birds-matrix.json). Two reads are a rule of the composer's and not a die of the cell's own (round K14): Gleam's chords read the tempo family, because the harmony pedal that holds the progression on its tonic requires the struck figure, and the figure opens only above 110 BPM; and Tide's section count reads whether the drums are on, because a theme under 96 bars gets its short outro only where it has a grid (R38). `says` is the long form (Eugene's copy to polish): one plain present-tense sentence of what the music is at that value, with the locked reading after it (`lockedSays`) — the explanation, the phone panel's line and the screen reader read nothing else, and tools/check.ts refuses a pole word in it. No genre is named anywhere in it (Eugene, 09-24: a genre word is not going to be accurate); a tempo family is said by what the kick does. `short` is the line the panel and the track show: the band's fact in five words at most, never the locked reading (that is the subtitle's) and never a genre; `says` stays the long form for the screen reader and a later info tip (Eugene, 09-24: \"more compact factual explanations that fit in 4-5 words\"). `lean` (round K15, Eugene: *\"we have at best 3–4 word variations per bird … could we fill in more text variants on bird movement\"*) is where in its band the value stands, off the same continuous quantity: the lower third says `lean.low`, the upper third `lean.high`, the middle the band's own — so a drag reads differently every sixth or so of the range while the word turns only at a threshold the composer has. `timbres` is each timbre id as it reads after \"The keys are …\" and on the short line (round K15: \"a vibes\" was an article by the first letter); tools/check.ts holds that every id the four keys lists can draw has one. Since K30 (the reviews of 09-26) a band's lines claim a part or a figure only where the program plays it: `{with}` in a line is filled from the band's `with` items whose `needs` fact the program shows (`programFacts`: the drum lanes' events, and the composition trace's selected parts and scene), joined by the band's `joiner`; a part the program does not play is left out, never said. Spark's `noDrums` is its word and lines with no drum event in the theme.",
